[{:db/id 17592186124646 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-6e0f0650-f17e-5833-8166-da3fd9367f23" "question-05d8ae1c-da42-5a43-aa56-47e108152b59" "question-dbf39e0a-85ba-5f58-ae9c-696c73732745" "question-5ccb6a2f-baf3-5789-838a-0e294ce49920"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124211 :question/problem "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(\\mathbf x)$ about $\\mathbf x = \\mathbf a\\in\\mathbb R^2$ is given by\n![](images/5e/5e93d9b7357f877e4c14dd90aa70de484fda76fd38a18151b8f7af60b84cd80f.svg)\n\nGiven that the eigenvalues of $\\mathbf{f}''(\\mathbf{a})$ are $\\lambda_1 = -1$ and $\\lambda_2 = -3,$ what type of point is $\\mathbf{a}?$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124379 :question/problem "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function ![](images/10/10b37b962af60201173147106bc48c82b09570a76e2bc357ce777befddbf8d2c.svg) about ![](images/e2/e25dfc8ddd2dd59ae418293416501c482448efb030358a30e270072685040d19.svg) is given by\n![](images/2f/2fae448e0c5f617b794c07e38d2563343163226542206cc5062343c9acc7f5f4.svg)\n\nwhere \n\n![](images/5d/5dbf02ec5ec0392e52f35435112cc4d65e89639c6cddbc3f15730075af3e85d4.svg) \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ![](images/4f/4f21c39d3dd4745d253d1f7251d2329915074e377086b7afcfe2eb14ebd692f8.svg) and ![](images/86/86c9216a6929cd7fad43c29575aa4ac8255863ce6d1df24eee2830224659b9e4.svg)\n\nII. ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite\n\nIII. ![](images/52/527e0abeb684dd24d8c92e45425542080c4c9493b9460bb89753fef3226c1f92.svg) is a saddle point of ![](images/bc/bc562d54615dad55d095db2afed7626f62ff7ebf0ab6c5d45471b77ac30b6398.svg)" :question/worked-solution "The point ![](images/1b/1b81872e7ebcaa9d0e229e5fbbe94b2a630d70400972d825cf8655961ffc7faa.svg) is a critical point of the function ![](images/35/35546b96dada825ebe9bf6a82803f85c40343497beb98e3b850067214a6e69ef.svg) if ![](images/6b/6bb3645c7e83b61a3945f1abd343897f1f70396e05762b9c80642a15f0d60387.svg) In such cases, we can classify ![](images/52/527e0abeb684dd24d8c92e45425542080c4c9493b9460bb89753fef3226c1f92.svg) as follows:\n\n- if ![](images/3d/3da86e43bb3fd2af8d7a2b53852a22e8231436da92b2c50cb03120493a621ba0.svg) is positive definite, then ![](images/52/527e0abeb684dd24d8c92e45425542080c4c9493b9460bb89753fef3226c1f92.svg) is a local minimum\n\n- if ![](images/3d/3da86e43bb3fd2af8d7a2b53852a22e8231436da92b2c50cb03120493a621ba0.svg) is negative definite, then ![](images/52/527e0abeb684dd24d8c92e45425542080c4c9493b9460bb89753fef3226c1f92.svg) is a local maximum\n\n- if ![](images/3d/3da86e43bb3fd2af8d7a2b53852a22e8231436da92b2c50cb03120493a621ba0.svg) is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then ![](images/52/527e0abeb684dd24d8c92e45425542080c4c9493b9460bb89753fef3226c1f92.svg) is a saddle point\n\nIn this case, we have ![](images/30/303d917136470ad212a0f4ac83f6861e8435742c764d929f632d541e0761c02f.svg) Now, before we examine the statements, we recall how to categorize quadratic forms.\n\nLet ![](images/c6/c6f4a56eed9073b3541e4398db9fc99f2e8ca94f651762e1f650bfe54865ca49.svg) for ![](images/16/1621ad9f5426c8fe19e2d5a00c5f92a60420aeaed7bfe623db70c9ab4e57527d.svg)  denote the leading principal minors of an ![](images/b0/b0cdde792f80dc6b0c5defb2f8d767ae115ecf922fa46f2a233f10959e509870.svg) matrix ![](images/c8/c8c72090d28b539bed2177052b06540383d63ac720008f1b301dafcac4fa6227.svg) Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ![](images/40/40138f7d0f5e38d137af53df63df0473cc827a5d8c8f957c7bd62d0ac2a30694.svg) (as well as the matrix ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg)) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ![](images/54/54ac4b9f53d36b439544b913f7ba0dcfb153aa76b9b9624632a41cd9392ce5ac.svg) for all ![](images/af/af25353ac60910a8cca7edfd3ea5312dea05646d41cd68c1cc45156c06391298.svg)\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all ![](images/af/af25353ac60910a8cca7edfd3ea5312dea05646d41cd68c1cc45156c06391298.svg) we have ![](images/03/039aff55ff839029e398761cdb94f09b8c2203413637d6f41ca9c680659cc8d2.svg) when ![](images/33/33f73a318016d781b6ae1aa913c2f1807ab5c7e6c2901e508fc56561fbe26b45.svg) is odd, and ![](images/54/54ac4b9f53d36b439544b913f7ba0dcfb153aa76b9b9624632a41cd9392ce5ac.svg) when ![](images/33/33f73a318016d781b6ae1aa913c2f1807ab5c7e6c2901e508fc56561fbe26b45.svg) is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, ![](images/0c/0cadce3122d5f1b1cb91927b6ab867404b826925c7a7900127b3824e7e286d2b.svg) but\n![](images/6e/6ee449b923787429cd51ad69f53e3420d1f50561840e54227a5cac671a7343cf.svg)\n\n- Statement II is true. Notice the following:\n![](images/2b/2bfb4950b5dc2d95b9328d8d923b66cdc19ff1608394aff61fb1ae7811ec8d21.svg)\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the matrix ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since the matrix ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, the critical point ![](images/52/527e0abeb684dd24d8c92e45425542080c4c9493b9460bb89753fef3226c1f92.svg) is a saddle point of ![](images/a0/a047a10a3ed3f5e86676e8fca21093abfe6a1849d90ce94b62101f34fca37e3d.svg)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-6e0f0650-f17e-5833-8166-da3fd9367f23" :question/id #uuid "6e0f0650-f17e-5833-8166-da3fd9367f23" :question/math-academy-id "q-163795" :question/problem "![](images/a3/a39696fcb202485b9a58839609ad807e43397fde8df965bed2824f6cdc307703.png)\n\nThe graph $z=f(x,y)$ is shown above. What are the global maximum points of the function?" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the following definitions:\n\n- The global maximum of a function is the largest value it attains on its domain. There can only be (at most) one global maximum.\n\n- A global maximum point is a point $(x,y,z)$ where a function reaches a global maximum. There can be zero, one, or many global maximum points.\n\nFor our function, the maximum value reached by the function is $f(x,y)=1.$ Therefore, the global maximum is $1.$ The function reaches this value when $(x,y)=(-1,-2)$ and  $(x,y)=(1,0),$so the global maximum points are $(-1,-2,1)$ and $(1,0,1).$\n\n![](images/4a/4a818d3e3e3c6c6a90ff9e69d808803016114894fbc605de5d21c75817eb8cf3.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-8390ddee-9042-5713-b922-461ba822e40f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-8390ddee-9042-5713-b922-461ba822e40f" :answer-field/id #uuid "8390ddee-9042-5713-b922-461ba822e40f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-cfc9d090-f059-5d47-bb5f-8ea074628146" :answer-field/choices ["answer-cfc9d090-f059-5d47-bb5f-8ea074628146" "answer-f027110e-c325-53d5-8b9d-70300263dd44" "answer-7c661f49-8f75-57c0-807f-bf1886abf88e" "answer-927103a8-b10f-5218-a094-fe0cd4985992" "answer-67ace240-ec76-51a3-9237-34363ff229dc"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cfc9d090-f059-5d47-bb5f-8ea074628146" :answer/id #uuid "cfc9d090-f059-5d47-bb5f-8ea074628146" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(-1,-2,1)$ and $(1,0,1)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f027110e-c325-53d5-8b9d-70300263dd44" :answer/id #uuid "f027110e-c325-53d5-8b9d-70300263dd44" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(-1,-2,1)$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7c661f49-8f75-57c0-807f-bf1886abf88e" :answer/id #uuid "7c661f49-8f75-57c0-807f-bf1886abf88e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(-1,2,-2.5)$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-927103a8-b10f-5218-a094-fe0cd4985992" :answer/id #uuid "927103a8-b10f-5218-a094-fe0cd4985992" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(1,0,1)$ and $(0,2,0)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-67ace240-ec76-51a3-9237-34363ff229dc" :answer/id #uuid "67ace240-ec76-51a3-9237-34363ff229dc" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(2,-3,0)$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-05d8ae1c-da42-5a43-aa56-47e108152b59" :question/id #uuid "05d8ae1c-da42-5a43-aa56-47e108152b59" :question/math-academy-id "q-163836" :question/problem "![](images/76/76cbc53a671cb7164352b2c71931379a6dab936a966ded05dc93ba31da013dfe.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $z=f(x,y)$ is shown above.\nWhat is the global minimum of the function?" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the following definitions:\n\n- The global minimum of a function is the smallest value it attains on its domain. There can only be (at most) one global minimum.\n\n- A global minimum point is a point $(x,y,z)$ where a function reaches a global minimum. There can be zero, one, or many global minimum points.\n\nFor our function, the minimum value reached by the function is $f(x,y)=-4.$ Therefore, the global minimum is $-4.$ The function reaches this value at both $(x,y)=(-3,-6)$ and $(x,y)=(-3,6).$\n\n![](images/78/78348020535973cddc75c2d935c0ed2f0390b5330b4139105be80cb8f6e6032a.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-17c926bd-dbcb-5faf-8bf3-0aee7298ed52"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-17c926bd-dbcb-5faf-8bf3-0aee7298ed52" :answer-field/id #uuid "17c926bd-dbcb-5faf-8bf3-0aee7298ed52" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-9386da2e-bffa-5ff6-8d56-e057722ac26a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-9386da2e-bffa-5ff6-8d56-e057722ac26a" "answer-e5115eb5-3e95-5d74-b63f-765192f9ac7a" "answer-9f6602ae-1519-5c89-b578-5c145b71a62c" "answer-085288bf-eb67-57c6-970e-1441a16de2cc" "answer-d0f6b593-67bc-5463-aa65-5339463d43d8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9386da2e-bffa-5ff6-8d56-e057722ac26a" :answer/id #uuid "9386da2e-bffa-5ff6-8d56-e057722ac26a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e5115eb5-3e95-5d74-b63f-765192f9ac7a" :answer/id #uuid "e5115eb5-3e95-5d74-b63f-765192f9ac7a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9f6602ae-1519-5c89-b578-5c145b71a62c" :answer/id #uuid "9f6602ae-1519-5c89-b578-5c145b71a62c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-085288bf-eb67-57c6-970e-1441a16de2cc" :answer/id #uuid "085288bf-eb67-57c6-970e-1441a16de2cc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d0f6b593-67bc-5463-aa65-5339463d43d8" :answer/id #uuid "d0f6b593-67bc-5463-aa65-5339463d43d8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-dbf39e0a-85ba-5f58-ae9c-696c73732745" :question/id #uuid "dbf39e0a-85ba-5f58-ae9c-696c73732745" :question/math-academy-id "q-163792" :question/problem "![](images/37/3764e985ef3b5c6b8c1bb5a3d347c52ef8a2a87edef3118c70da98f92fbd5d00.png)\n\nThe graph $z=f(x,y)$ is shown above. What are the global minimum points of the function?" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the following definitions:\n\n- The global minimum of a function is the smallest value it attains on its domain. There can only be (at most) one global minimum.\n\n- A global minimum point is a point $(x,y,z)$ where a function reaches a global minimum. There can be zero, one, or many global minimum points.\n\nFor our function, the minimum value reached by the function is $f(x,y)=-3.$ Therefore, the global minimum is $-3.$ The function reaches this value when $(x,y)=(1,-1),$ and  $(x,y)=(-1,2),$ so the global minimum points are $(1,-1,-3)$ and $(-1,2,-3).$\n\n![](images/7d/7d62c7d1b3a265608fd93f395612092e1643858b1515f0e4d7a313b82b4c8f78.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-4dbc2da6-1c92-5d18-8912-6e342b2f7cab"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-4dbc2da6-1c92-5d18-8912-6e342b2f7cab" :answer-field/id #uuid "4dbc2da6-1c92-5d18-8912-6e342b2f7cab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-2390596a-df7b-5124-a17c-f037210598e2" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c7c277f6-24c3-5b09-85a8-c9d95a733c43" "answer-37d1cb39-5327-5d1b-968d-afe88f5dcd13" "answer-2390596a-df7b-5124-a17c-f037210598e2" "answer-9c3a7d7b-6f40-5e6b-a3e0-f0eaa1ec0743" "answer-da72bc2c-d100-558f-9df8-60fb81019a26"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c7c277f6-24c3-5b09-85a8-c9d95a733c43" :answer/id #uuid "c7c277f6-24c3-5b09-85a8-c9d95a733c43" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(-1,2,-3)$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-37d1cb39-5327-5d1b-968d-afe88f5dcd13" :answer/id #uuid "37d1cb39-5327-5d1b-968d-afe88f5dcd13" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(1,-1,-3)$ and  $(0,-3,0)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2390596a-df7b-5124-a17c-f037210598e2" :answer/id #uuid "2390596a-df7b-5124-a17c-f037210598e2" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(1,-1,-3)$ and $(-1,2,-3)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9c3a7d7b-6f40-5e6b-a3e0-f0eaa1ec0743" :answer/id #uuid "9c3a7d7b-6f40-5e6b-a3e0-f0eaa1ec0743" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(1,-1,-3)$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-da72bc2c-d100-558f-9df8-60fb81019a26" :answer/id #uuid "da72bc2c-d100-558f-9df8-60fb81019a26" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(1,2,0)$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-5ccb6a2f-baf3-5789-838a-0e294ce49920" :question/id #uuid "5ccb6a2f-baf3-5789-838a-0e294ce49920" :question/math-academy-id "q-163794" :question/problem "![](images/f8/f82404522255a2e5b8be8b45d39062bd2239c15294024c02d6d4ad3dd3f44809.png)\n\nThe graph $z=f(x,y)$ is shown above. What are the global minimum points of the function?" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the following definitions:\n\n- The global minimum of a function is the smallest value it attains on its domain. There can only be (at most) one global minimum.\n\n- A global minimum point is a point $(x,y,z)$ where a function reaches a global minimum. There can be zero, one, or many global minimum points.\n\nFor our function, the minimum value reached by the function is $f(x,y)=-2.5.$ Therefore, the global minimum is $-2.5.$ The function reaches this value when $(x,y)=(-1,2),$ so the global minimum point is $(-1,2,-2.5).$\n\n![](images/29/29d2b703b9c17aa69bb41076e4bf40f509af4b44bd3ade14dbdeaec7cad25120.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-7e92c66f-883a-5be4-b481-34094dc1948a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7e92c66f-883a-5be4-b481-34094dc1948a" :answer-field/id #uuid "7e92c66f-883a-5be4-b481-34094dc1948a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-c1664007-041b-58fe-aa78-e1585e47911c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-eedfc3db-8fe3-54f8-8907-da861cf5c513" "answer-48052d29-4422-5f9a-b0b0-95c416d880f9" "answer-c1664007-041b-58fe-aa78-e1585e47911c" "answer-981d803b-2b71-589c-9dd7-89b262f35507" "answer-0c750366-87fd-5df1-9550-7377df9d1451"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-eedfc3db-8fe3-54f8-8907-da861cf5c513" :answer/id #uuid "eedfc3db-8fe3-54f8-8907-da861cf5c513" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(-1,2,-2.5)$ and $(-1,-2,2)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-48052d29-4422-5f9a-b0b0-95c416d880f9" :answer/id #uuid "48052d29-4422-5f9a-b0b0-95c416d880f9" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(-1,2,-2.5)$ and $(1,0,-1)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c1664007-041b-58fe-aa78-e1585e47911c" :answer/id #uuid "c1664007-041b-58fe-aa78-e1585e47911c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(-1,2,-2.5)$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-981d803b-2b71-589c-9dd7-89b262f35507" :answer/id #uuid "981d803b-2b71-589c-9dd7-89b262f35507" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(1,0,-1)$ and $(2,-3,0)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0c750366-87fd-5df1-9550-7377df9d1451" :answer/id #uuid "0c750366-87fd-5df1-9550-7377df9d1451" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(1,0,-1)$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186125817 :tutorial/content "Recall that the *derivative* of a multivariable function $f:\\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R$ at $\\mathbf x = \\mathbf a$ is given by the matrix\n\n![](images/b5/b5dfe5fbad89ef3c6231dafe40d719be1faf007f782342abd6475756eda97b1e.svg)\n\nAnalogously with functions of one variable, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point $\\mathbf x = \\mathbf a$ is a **critical point** of $f$ if $\\boldsymbol f'(\\mathbf a) = \\mathbf 0^T$ or does not exist.\n\n- Critical points where $\\boldsymbol f'(\\mathbf a) = \\mathbf 0^T$ are known as **stationary points.**\n\nThe local extrema of a function $f$ occur at the critical points. So, we can find the local extrema of $f$ by determining the stationary points *and* the points where $\\boldsymbol f'(\\mathbf x)$ does not exist and classify them accordingly (we'll discuss how to do this shortly). \n\nNot all of the solutions of $\\boldsymbol f'(\\mathbf a) = \\mathbf 0^T$ give rise to local extrema! Solutions to $\\boldsymbol f'(\\mathbf a) = \\mathbf 0^T$ that *do not* give rise to local extreme values are called **saddle points**.\n\nFor example, consider the hyperbolic paraboloid \n\n$$\nz = f(x,y) = y^2-x^2\n$$\n\nwhose graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/ce/cea092da684db6b055c78369fcb987c3577a027ef0d8f03d598e115e85775ff5.png)\n\nIt's easy to check that $\\boldsymbol f'(0,0) = \\mathbf 0^T,$ and so $(0,0)$ is a stationary point of $f.$ This point is a saddle point. Note that the surface curves up in one direction (the red curve) and down in a different direction (the green curve), resembling a horse riding saddle.\n\nIn any neighborhood of a saddle point $\\mathbf a,$ there are points $\\mathbf{x}$ and $\\mathbf{x}'$ such that $\\mathbf{x}-\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{x}'-\\mathbf{a}$ are orthogonal and they satisfy\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf{x}) - f(\\mathbf a) >0 \\quad \\textrm{and} \\quad f(\\mathbf{x}') - f(\\mathbf a) <0.\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186125816 :tutorial/content "So, given a stationary critical point $\\mathbf{a} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n$ of a function $f : \\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R},$ how do we classify it?\n\nOne way is to consider the Taylor expansion of $f$ about $\\mathbf{a}{:}$\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf{x}) = f(\\mathbf{a}) + \\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf{a})(\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{a}) + \\dfrac12(\\mathbf{x}-\\mathbf{a})^T \\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf{a})(\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{a}) + \\cdots\n$$\n\nSince $\\mathbf{a}$ is a stationary point, we have $\\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf{a}) = \\mathbf 0^T.$ This gives\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf{x}) = f(\\mathbf{a})  + \\dfrac12(\\mathbf{x}-\\mathbf{a})^T \\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf{a})(\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{a}) + \\cdots.\n$$\n\nLet's assume that $\\mathbf x$ is close to $\\mathbf a,$ $\\det\\left(\\boldsymbol f''(\\mathbf a)\\right) \\neq 0,$ and that the terms of cubic order and higher are sufficiently small. Then, we have\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf{x}) \\approx f(\\mathbf{a})  + \\dfrac12(\\mathbf{x}-\\mathbf{a})^T \\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf{a})(\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{a})\n$$\n\nwhich we can rewrite as\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf{x}) - f(\\mathbf{a}) \\approx \\dfrac12(\\mathbf{x}-\\mathbf{a})^T \\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf{a})(\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{a}).\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf{a})$ is an $n \\times n$ *symmetric* matrix. Thus, the term\n\n$$\n(\\mathbf{x}-\\mathbf{a})^T \\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf{a})(\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{a})\n$$\n\nis a *quadratic form*.\n\nTherefore, we note the following:\n\n- If $\\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf{a})$ is positive definite, this quadratic form is always positive, and\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf{x}) - f(\\mathbf{a}) \\approx \\dfrac12(\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{a})^T \\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf{a})(\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{a}) > 0\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\nf(\\mathbf{x}) > f(\\mathbf{a})\n$$\n\nfor all $\\mathbf{x}$ sufficiently close to $\\mathbf{a}.$ Hence, $\\mathbf{a}$ is a local *minimum*.\n\n- If $\\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf{a})$ is negative definite, this quadratic form is always negative, and\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf{x}) - f(\\mathbf{a}) \\approx \\dfrac12(\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{a})^T \\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf{a})(\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{a}) < 0\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\nf(\\mathbf{x}) < f(\\mathbf{a})\n$$\n\nfor all $\\mathbf{x}$ sufficiently close to $\\mathbf{a}.$ Hence, $\\mathbf{a}$ is a local *maximum.*\n\n- If this quadratic form is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $\\mathbf{a}$ is a saddle point.\n\nThe assumption $\\det\\left(\\boldsymbol f''(\\mathbf a)\\right) \\neq 0$ is important. If $\\det\\left(\\boldsymbol f''(\\mathbf a)\\right) =0,$ then the quadratic form is zero along some line through $\\mathbf a$ or zero *everywhere* in a neighborhood of $\\mathbf a.$ In such cases, we cannot ignore the higher-order terms and must use another method to classify the critical point.\n\nIn the work that follows, we will only consider cases where $\\det\\left(\\boldsymbol f''(\\mathbf a)\\right) \\neq 0.$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124736 :activity/expected-seconds 660.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
